  "id": "COMPOUND:16888 - PROTEIN:3781",
  "verbalization": "Theasaponin E1 appears to have a shared target relationship with C-C chemokine receptor type 2 through a chain of targets involving 4-(3-Pyridin-2-Yl-1h-Pyrazol-4-Yl)Quinoline. This quinoline compound is identified as a target of multiple kinases, including Tyrosine-protein kinase Blk, Phosphatidylinositol 4-phosphate 3-kinase C2 domain-containing subunit beta, and Serine/threonine-protein kinase 33. All three of these kinases are implicated in the signaling pathways that ultimately lead to the activation of C-C chemokine receptors. The consistent involvement of this quinoline compound in targeting C-C chemokine receptors suggests a potential mechanism by which Theasaponin E1 exerts its effects, possibly through modulation of these signaling pathways. The evidence supports a direct or indirect link between Theasaponin E1 and C-C chemokine receptor type 2, mediated through the quinoline compound and its downstream targets.",
